// ----- The parse context: in a generator, in a function -----
//
// Three static-semantics rules of JavaScript depend on WHERE a construct stands rather
// than on what it looks like:
//   * inside a generator, 'yield' is a reserved word - 'function *g(){ var yield }',
//     'function *g(yield){}' and 'catch (yield)' are all syntax errors;
//   * outside a generator, 'yield' is an ordinary identifier, so 'yield v' is not a
//     yield expression but two expressions in a row (which ASI then refuses);
//   * 'return' may only appear inside a function.
//
// All three are answered by ONE walk of the source prefix, which each guard below
// carries its own copy of - a :script runs in the parser VM and cannot call a helper
// defined in the grammar's start script, and the three differ only in the final test.
// The walk's answer is a PURE function of the source prefix, for the same reason
// NotStrict's is (see there): the parser backtracks constantly and nothing would undo a
// flag set by a rolled-back alternative. Its end state is memoized by position, which
// a backtrack cannot invalidate.
//
// It tracks a bracket stack. A '(' preceded by a 'function' head (with or without a '*')
// opens a parameter list, and its ')' hands the generator flag on to the body '{' that
// follows; a '(' preceded by if/while/for/switch/catch/with opens a control-statement
// head, whose block is not a function body; anything else - a method head, an arrow's
// parameter list - is a plain function head. A '=>' marks the body after it as an arrow
// body, which is a function body that INHERITS the enclosing generator flag.
//
// The deliberate approximations, all of which can only misjudge a file that already
